{
  "claims": [
    {
      "claim_id": "c1",
      "verdict": "SUPPORTED",
      "reasoning": "Passage [0] states that all processes using a database must be on the same host computer, and passage [1] explains that the wal-index is maintained in shared memory, requiring all readers to be on the same machine.",
      "citations": [
        {"index": 0, "quote": "All processes using a database must be on the same host computer; WAL does not work over a network filesystem."},
        {"index": 1, "quote": "the use of shared memory means that all readers must exist on the same machine."}
      ]
    },
    {
      "claim_id": "c2",
      "verdict": "SUPPORTED",
      "reasoning": "Passage [0] explicitly states that WAL does not work over a network filesystem, which implies that two processes on different machines sharing a database over NFS cannot use WAL.",
      "citations": [
        {"index": 0, "quote": "WAL does not work over a network filesystem."}
      ]
    },
    {
      "claim_id": "c3",
      "verdict": "CONTRADICTED",
      "reasoning": "Passage [3] shows that WAL can work without shared memory if EXCLUSIVE locking mode is set, and passage [5] shows that if shared memory is unavailable and NORMAL locking mode is used, WAL access fails. Thus, being on the same host is not sufficient; the locking mode and shared memory support also matter.",
      "citations": [
        {"index": 3, "quote": "WAL databases can be created, read, and written even if shared memory is unavailable as long as the locking_mode is set to EXCLUSIVE before the first attempted access."},
        {"index": 5, "quote": "If NORMAL locking mode is in effect for the first WAL-mode database access, then the shared-memory wal-index is created. This means that the underlying VFS must support the 'version 2' shared-memory. If the VFS does not support shared-memory methods, then the attempt to open a database that is already in WAL mode, or the attempt to convert a database into WAL mode, will fail."}
      ]
    },
    {
      "claim_id": "c4",
      "verdict": "SUPPORTED",
      "reasoning": "Passage [2] states that there can only be one writer at a time because there is only one WAL file.",
      "citations": [
        {"index": 2, "quote": "since there is only one WAL file, there can only be one writer at a time."}
      ]
    },
    {
      "claim_id": "c5",
      "verdict": "SUPPORTED",
      "reasoning": "Passage [2] explicitly states that writers and readers can run at the same time.",
      "citations": [
        {"index": 2, "quote": "Because writers do nothing that would interfere with the actions of readers, writers and readers can run at the same time."}
      ]
    },
    {
      "claim_id": "c6",
      "verdict": "UNSUPPORTED",
      "reasoning": "The passages do not mention BEGIN CONCURRENT or any way to lift the single-writer limit.",
      "citations": []
    },
    {
      "claim_id": "c7",
      "verdict": "SUPPORTED",
      "reasoning": "Passage [3] states that a process can interact with a WAL database without shared memory if it is guaranteed to be the only process accessing the database, and this is achieved by setting locking_mode to EXCLUSIVE before the first attempted access.",
      "citations": [
        {"index": 3, "quote": "a process can interact with a WAL database without using shared memory if that process is guaranteed to be the only process accessing the database."},
        {"index": 3, "quote": "as long as the locking_mode is set to EXCLUSIVE before the first attempted access."}
      ]
    },
    {
      "claim_id": "c8",
      "verdict": "SUPPORTED",
      "reasoning": "Passage [4] states that if EXCLUSIVE locking mode is set prior to the first WAL-mode database access, the database connection remains in EXCLUSIVE mode as long as the journal mode is WAL, and the only way to change out of EXCLUSIVE is to first change out of WAL journal mode.",
      "citations": [
        {"index": 4, "quote": "the database connection remains in EXCLUSIVE mode as long as the journal mode is WAL; attempts to change the locking mode using 'PRAGMA locking_mode=NORMAL;' are no-ops. The only way to change out of EXCLUSIVE locking mode is to first change out of WAL journal mode."}
      ]
    },
    {
      "claim_id": "c9",
      "verdict": "SUPPORTED",
      "reasoning": "Passage [6] states that with PRAGMA synchronous set to NORMAL, writers omit the sync on every transaction commit.",
      "citations": [
        {"index": 6, "quote": "Writers sync the WAL on every transaction commit if PRAGMA synchronous is set to FULL but omit this sync if PRAGMA synchronous is set to NORMAL."}
      ]
    },
    {
      "claim_id": "c10",
      "verdict": "SUPPORTED",
      "reasoning": "Passage [7] explicitly states that with PRAGMA synchronous set to NORMAL, the checkpoint is the only operation to issue an I/O barrier or sync operation.",
      "citations": [
        {"index": 7, "quote": "with PRAGMA synchronous set to NORMAL, the checkpoint is the only operation to issue an I/O barrier or sync operation (fsync() on unix or FlushFileBuffers() on windows)."}
      ]
    },
    {
      "claim_id": "c11",
      "verdict": "SUPPORTED",
      "reasoning": "Passage [6] states that writers sync the WAL on every transaction commit if PRAGMA synchronous is set to FULL.",
      "citations": [
        {"index": 6, "quote": "Writers sync the WAL on every transaction commit if PRAGMA synchronous is set to FULL"}
      ]
    },
    {
      "claim_id": "c12",
      "verdict": "CONTRADICTED",
      "reasoning": "Passage [7] says that if the application runs checkpoint in a separate thread or process, the main thread will never block on a sync operation, but this is specifically under the condition that PRAGMA synchronous is set to NORMAL. The claim says 'whatever the synchronous setting', which is not supported; with FULL, commits do sync, so the main thread could block.",
      "citations": [
        {"index": 7, "quote": "Note that with PRAGMA synchronous set to NORMAL, the checkpoint is the only operation to issue an I/O barrier or sync operation ... If an application therefore runs checkpoint in a separate thread or process, the main thread or process that is doing database queries and updates will never block on a sync operation."}
      ]
    }
  ]
}

{
  "claims": [
    {
      "claim_id": "c1",
      "verdict": "SUPPORTED",
      "reasoning": "The passage explicitly states that incrementing a counter 100 times results in 100 entries in the AOF.",
      "citations": [
        {"index": 3, "quote": "if you are incrementing a counter 100 times, you'll end up with a single key in your dataset containing the final value, but 100 entries in your AOF."}
      ]
    },
    {
      "claim_id": "c2",
      "verdict": "SUPPORTED",
      "reasoning": "The passage states that 99 of those 100 entries are not needed to rebuild the current state.",
      "citations": [
        {"index": 3, "quote": "99 of those entries are not needed to rebuild the current state."}
      ]
    },
    {
      "claim_id": "c3",
      "verdict": "SUPPORTED",
      "reasoning": "The passage explains that BGREWRITEAOF writes the shortest sequence of commands needed to rebuild the current dataset, implying the counter can be rebuilt with a single command.",
      "citations": [
        {"index": 4, "quote": "Redis will write the shortest sequence of commands needed to rebuild the current dataset in memory."}
      ]
    },
    {
      "claim_id": "c4",
      "verdict": "SUPPORTED",
      "reasoning": "The passage explicitly states that on Redis 2.2 you need to run BGREWRITEAOF from time to time.",
      "citations": [
        {"index": 4, "quote": "If you're using the AOF with Redis 2.2 you'll need to run `BGREWRITEAOF` from time to time."}
      ]
    },
    {
      "claim_id": "c5",
      "verdict": "UNSUPPORTED",
      "reasoning": "The passage mentions Redis 2.4 can trigger log rewriting automatically, but does not mention Redis 2.6.",
      "citations": []
    },
    {
      "claim_id": "c6",
      "verdict": "SUPPORTED",
      "reasoning": "The passage states that Redis >= 2.4 avoids triggering an AOF rewrite when an RDB snapshotting is in progress, and prevents BGSAVE during AOF rewrite, meaning they never run at the same time.",
      "citations": [
        {"index": 7, "quote": "Redis >= 2.4 makes sure to avoid triggering an AOF rewrite when an RDB snapshotting operation is already in progress, or allowing a `BGSAVE` while the AOF rewrite is in progress."}
      ]
    },
    {
      "claim_id": "c7",
      "verdict": "SUPPORTED",
      "reasoning": "The passage explicitly states that if BGREWRITEAOF is requested during snapshotting, the server replies OK and schedules the rewrite to start after snapshotting completes.",
      "citations": [
        {"index": 8, "quote": "When snapshotting is in progress and the user explicitly requests a log rewrite operation using `BGREWRITEAOF` the server will reply with an OK status code telling the user the operation is scheduled, and the rewrite will start once the snapshotting is completed."}
      ]
    },
    {
      "claim_id": "c8",
      "verdict": "SUPPORTED",
      "reasoning": "The passage states that with appendfsync everysec, you may lose 1 second of data if there is a disaster.",
      "citations": [
        {"index": 5, "quote": "`appendfsync everysec`: `fsync` every second. Fast enough (since version 2.4 likely to be as fast as snapshotting), and you may lose 1 second of data if there is a disaster."}
      ]
    },
    {
      "claim_id": "c9",
      "verdict": "UNSUPPORTED",
      "reasoning": "The passage describes appendfsync always as 'very safe' but does not claim that no writes are lost in any failure scenario.",
      "citations": []
